The schema-bound prefix checkpoint, on
f280b26983ad0fdb705a0d9ebf0503e76f2899b0 after the eight-patch production
series.

A served web turn carries a stable head: the chat template's preamble, the
system prompt, and the tool schemas the page composes from GET /tools. At
f280b269 that head is reused across turns of one conversation and, under narrow
conditions, across conversations that meet the same slot in sequence. The
narrowness is the whole subject. Qwen3.5 is hybrid, so
llama_memory_hybrid::seq_pos_min returns the max over its two halves and the
recurrent half reports one cell at the latest position; the rollback
update_slots needs therefore always enters the checkpoint search at
server-context.cpp:3233, and the only thing that keeps a diverging request off
the do_reset branch at 3263 is a surviving checkpoint at or below the
divergence point. Those checkpoints are saved with
LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY (2247), so each carries the recurrent half
alone and reads the attention cells below its boundary out of a slot that still
holds them, and the erase loop at 3274 removes every checkpoint above pos_next.
One request whose prefix diverges below the head therefore takes the whole head
with it: pos_next reaches 0, every checkpoint is erased, the memory is cleared
at 3328, and the next request that shares the head reprefills it from token 0.

This patch retains one whole sequence state at the boundary of the last user
message and restores it for a later request whose token array starts with the
pinned prefix and extends it. It is saved with LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_NONE, so it
carries the attention cells and the recurrent state together and a restore
reconstructs the prefix from the blob rather than from what the slot happens to
still hold. The restore therefore runs after slot.prompt_clear(), which makes
the sequence empty before the blob is written and leaves an empty sequence
rather than a mixed one where the write fails.

The capture proves its own invariant rather than inheriting one. A blob is only
the prefix's state where the memory holds exactly the tokens the pinned array
will carry, so the capture requires llama_memory_seq_pos_max to equal
n_tokens_start - 1 at the site where create_checkpoint settles for a pos_min and
pos_max that are merely consistent with each other. A capture whose recurrent
state sat one batch ahead of its own token array would restore an answer rather
than fail, which is the hazard the whole-state save exists to close.

The key is a SHA-256 over the fields the retained bytes depend on: the model
path and the loader's description of what it holds, a digest of the chat
template's default and tool_use sources, the tuple that decides the layout of
the state bytes (n_ctx, n_batch, n_ubatch, both cache types, the flash attention
setting, the checkpoint count and its minimum spacing), the build identity, a
digest of the tokens of the system span, and a digest of the whole prefix token
array beside its length. Each field is length-prefixed, so a byte moved across a
field boundary changes the key. The key labels the pin and the verbatim token
comparison in server_prefix_checkpoint::covers guards it, so a digest collision
moves no state. The tool schemas enter the token array through the Jinja
template and reach no field of their own: where the template renders them inside
the system block the system span digest carries them, and where it renders them
elsewhere the prefix token digest does. Both are fields of the key.

Message spans decide whether the mechanism engages at all. server-context.cpp
reads message_delimiters out of the request body, server-common.cpp writes it
there from chat_params.message_delimiters on the OAI-compatible chat path alone,
and the specialized template handlers in common/chat.cpp fill that field. A
request carrying no delimiters places no user start anywhere, so no batch
boundary lands at the head, and this patch captures nothing and restores
nothing. It shares that dependence with the context checkpoints already in the
file.

QWEN_PREFIX_CHECKPOINT arms it. An unset, empty, or "0" value leaves every hook
inert and the file behaves as it did, which is the state of every llama-bench,
probe, and harness in this tree. The mechanism is independent of
--ctx-checkpoints: the fill loop's break at the last user message is what puts a
batch boundary at the pin's own boundary, and the patch admits that break when
the pin is armed while leaving the do_checkpoint spacing rule untouched. A build
carrying this patch therefore measures the pin at --ctx-checkpoints 0, which is
what qwen-capacity-policy.sh admits for a build whose server-context.cpp digest
moves the checkpoint_semantics declaration to unknown.

Arming the pin changes the prompt fill partition where the count is 0, since a
batch then ends at the last user message that would otherwise have run on. An
armed run and an unarmed run are two execution shapes, so the control for a
measurement is an armed run whose prefix the pin does not hold rather than an
unarmed run.

The hunk offsets encode the post-series line numbers, so the patch applies after
llama-vulkan-low-priority, llama-no-cpu-fallback, llama-vulkan-duty-cycle,
llama-vulkan-runtime-submit-limit, llama-vulkan-submit-trace,
llama-router-tools-proxy, llama-vulkan-view-alias-deps, and
llama-server-natural-checkpoint-boundary, in that order.

